systemd: fall back to /proc when faccessat cannot take AT_EMPTY_PATH

With 0007 applied, PID 1 mounts cgroup2, prints its version banner for the first
time on this device and gets as far as setting the hostname before dying.
Measured on dandelion (Linux 4.9.190), stage-2 boot of 2026-08-06, read out of
the raw log ring:

  [ 30.357571] systemd[1]: Mounting cgroup2 with
               "nsdelegate,memory_recursiveprot" failed, retrying without
               mount options.
  [ 30.755152] systemd[1]: systemd 261 running in system mode
  [ 30.822073] systemd[1]: Detected architecture arm64.
  [ 30.822552] systemd[1]: Detected first boot.
  [ 30.980940] systemd[1]: Hostname set to <thompson>.
  [ 31.031544] systemd[1]: Failed to allocate manager object: Invalid argument
  [ 31.114951] systemd[1]: Freezing execution.

That 0007 works is the first half of the evidence: mount_setup() completed and
manager_new() is a later caller entirely.

The failure is manager_new() -> pin_callout_binary() -> find_executable_full() ->
open_and_check_executable() -> access_fd(), which calls

    faccessat(fd, "", mode, AT_EMPTY_PATH)

faccessat2() is Linux 5.8. Where it is missing the syscall answers -ENOSYS, and
glibc falls back to emulating the four-argument faccessat() in userspace -- an
emulation that rejects every flag outside AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW and AT_EACCESS
with -EINVAL, AT_EMPTY_PATH among them. So the error does not mean "the mode is
wrong", it means "the libc could not ask the kernel". Nothing between
open_and_check_executable() and main.c looks at -EINVAL, and the message is
emitted by log_debug_errno(), so at the default log level the freeze arrives
with no indication of which step produced it.

Note the caller already handles the case where this check cannot be performed:

    r = access_fd(fd, X_OK);
    if (r == -ENOSYS)
            /* /proc/ is not mounted. Fall back to access(). */

so /proc/ is the route it expects, and -ENOSYS is the signal it understands.
This patch takes that route on -EINVAL as well, and reports -ENOSYS when /proc/
turns out not to be mounted after all, so the existing fallback still fires.

Retry rather than probe, and only after a failure, so a kernel with faccessat2()
never reaches the added code. Compare fchmodat() a few functions above in the
same file, which already treats an EINVAL from glibc's flag validation as
"glibc too old?" and falls through to the raw syscall -- the shape here is that
one, with /proc/ standing in for the syscall that does not exist yet.

--- a/src/basic/fs-util.c
+++ b/src/basic/fs-util.c
@@ -372,4 +372,18 @@
         assert(fd >= 0);
 
-        return RET_NERRNO(faccessat(fd, "", mode, AT_EMPTY_PATH));
+        int r = RET_NERRNO(faccessat(fd, "", mode, AT_EMPTY_PATH));
+        if (r != -EINVAL)
+                return r;
+
+        /* faccessat2() is Linux 5.8. Without it glibc emulates the four-argument
+         * faccessat() in userspace and rejects any flag outside AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW and
+         * AT_EACCESS with -EINVAL, AT_EMPTY_PATH included -- so this is "the libc could
+         * not ask", not "the mode is wrong". Go through /proc/ instead, which is the
+         * route the callers already assume exists when they special-case -ENOSYS, and
+         * answer -ENOSYS if it turns out not to be mounted so that fallback still runs. */
+        r = RET_NERRNO(access(FORMAT_PROC_FD_PATH(fd), mode));
+        if (r == -ENOENT && proc_mounted() == 0)
+                return -ENOSYS;
+
+        return r;
 }
 